  "worked_solution": "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2033}+P(x){y}^{\u2032}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{\u2032}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{\u2032}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=-3{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{\u2032}(x)=3{x}^{2}W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{\u2032} & =3{x}^{2}W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =3{x}^{2}\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int 3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}\u2061|W| & ={x}^{3}+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{e}^{{x}^{3}} \\\\ W & =C{e}^{{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(1)=1$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(1) & =C{e}^{{1}^{3}} \\\\ 1 & =Ce \\\\ C & ={e}^{-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nW(x)={e}^{-1}{e}^{{x}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $W(2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(2) & ={e}^{-1}{e}^{{2}^{3}}={e}^{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
